id #edb/ref 17592186556498} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdc48711-9294-5620-8821-e5c664e49e7f" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3412a484-45d8-574d-9d62-4ede649dfd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556541} {:answer/id #uuid "35c4e0e7-1d76-539c-97b6-f431a62e393c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556546} {:answer/id #uuid "55d344b2-0d9d-5c9e-9649-438f542aaff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556628} {:answer/id #uuid "83cd1bb7-6b3e-5f38-831e-6c8449b88613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556738} {:answer/id #uuid "d1852c5e-8a51-5e17-8a30-b0f014c72c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556628} :answer-field/id #uuid "d735f931-6366-5aec-9f2f-070c16532ce5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79cf6220-8640-5993-92c2-8195fa289578" :question/math-academy-id "q-335009" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.633,\\,2.784,\\,3.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556440} {:answer/id #uuid "23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.546,\\,1.699,\\,2.335" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556513} {:answer/id #uuid "856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.940,\\,2.493,\\,3.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556745} {:answer/id #uuid "992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.443,\\,2.564,\\,3.671" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556799} {:answer/id #uuid "a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.021,\\,1.076,\\,2.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556833} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147776" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.041,\\,0.137,\\,0.782\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $x\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\ge 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =1-y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.041,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.041}}≈1.021$\n\n- For $y=0.137,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.137}}≈1.076$\n\n- For $y=0.782,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.782}}≈2.142$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c8a159-2ab7-583f-b9ca-e09e33a5305f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.095,\\,-2.423,\\,-1.956" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556533} {:answer/id #uuid "49f3228a-be25-54a2-b493-12bafe2ab3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.657,\\,-2.134,\\,-2.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556596} {:answer/id #uuid "78ff442a-3704-51bf-96bc-0abf7085c311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.768,\\,-0.773,\\,-0.231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556708} {:answer/id #uuid "8cde8170-c21d-53f8-bce9-7b3b74453420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.189,\\,-0.787,\\,-0.223" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556769} {:answer/id #uuid "e55cf5bc-1685-5922-8164-3a84b98c019b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.998,\\,-1.067,\\,-0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e896c598-93c0-535b-bc5e-efc7006916d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be496a82-fb90-5b74-9be3-d9e0bf10ddb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-147598" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.112,\\,0.455,\\,0.800\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & \\,x\\le 0, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\le 0$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.112,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.112)≈-2.189$\n\n- For $y=0.455,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.455)≈-0.787$\n\n- For $y=0.800,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.800)≈-0.223$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ffc6693-7918-5b27-a0b4-e3a0783bca18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.502,\\,0.798,\\,0.904" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556685} {:answer/id #uuid "8420c9eb-cfa9-54a2-86e8-b718c39b55a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.467,\\,0.623,\\,0.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556741} {:answer/id #uuid "88f1ede4-d25a-58dc-a83e-6b6a90d5dafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.636,\\,0.793,\\,0.858" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556753} {:answer/id #uuid "942bdd27-dcc3-5986-a885-d194ecacc967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.221,\\,0.467,\\,0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556787} {:answer/id #uuid "e39fbb91-ba93-50b2-be45-d43aa8372324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281,\\,0.546,\\,0.771" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556753} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c6038e-d575-5102-98ac-e0a7869bfe80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9e69354-0b9b-54c9-b814-cf1944d62671" :question/math-academy-id "q-147659" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.257,\\,0.498,\\,0.632\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.257,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.257}≈0.636$\n\n- For $y=0.498,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.498}≈0.793$\n\n- For $y=0.632,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.632}≈0.858$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01632674-02ee-5ccc-844f-443846c79b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intesecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556432} {:answer/id #uuid "08c8a772-ae09-512b-a890-92df6d1b8e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556449} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2cc041-c79e-5e86-a8d4-d48bf4faa510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556773} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8c1e08-78ee-5f92-b589-90b9f4d1a083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556776} {:answer/id #uuid "dd035faa-00da-5a95-8985-5b4ebe31a5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556773} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b32496-c7c6-5ba5-9d57-aa0f0c7fc355" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f46d525e-1765-5c37-bb92-276f989431a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96069" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=13\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-3({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2}+3 & =13 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1 \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$"}]}
